        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best jump rope for beginners?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A rope that gives you feedback while you learn the rhythm: a lightly weighted rope such as the Crossrope Get Lean, a beaded rope, or an inexpensive bearing rope such as the Loocio, the DEGOL-style rope we found on Amazon. Very fast, thin speed ropes are harder to time. If your ceiling is low or you are worried about tripping, start with a cordless trainer.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I know what size jump rope to get?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Stand on the middle of the rope with one foot and pull the handles up; for most beginners the top of the cable should reach about the armpits, and experienced jumpers often go shorter. For fixed-length ropes, use the maker's height chart. With a cut-to-length cable, shorten it a little at a time and jump before cutting, because you cannot add cable back.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are weighted jump ropes better?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not better, different. A rope with a heavier cable turns more slowly, which makes rhythm easier and works the shoulders and grip harder. Weight in the handles adds arm work without slowing the rope. Light speed ropes are better for double-unders and long, fast sets. Many people use a weighted rope for conditioning and a speed rope for skill.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you jump rope on concrete?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              You can, but it is hard on both the rope and your legs. Rough concrete and asphalt wear the plastic coating off cable ropes, and makers including WOD Nation and Crossrope recommend smooth surfaces or a mat. A firm rubber mat or gym flooring is better for your joints too. Beaded ropes cope with rough surfaces better than coated cables.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How many calories does jumping rope burn?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Roughly 11 to 17 calories per minute of continuous skipping for people weighing 130 to 200 lb, based on the Compendium of Physical Activities value of 11 METs. That is about 115 to 175 calories for ten minutes of non-stop jumping. These are estimates; real sessions include pauses, and smart rope and listing figures are estimates too.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How high does a ceiling need to be for jumping rope?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              There is no single figure, because it depends on your height, rope length and how high you jump. Test it: stand where you will jump and turn the rope slowly over your head without jumping. If it touches or comes close to the ceiling, a light or a fan, the room is too low and a cordless rope is the better choice.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

